    { q: "What is the purpose of 'peer review' in the writing process?", options: ["To have others fix all your mistakes for you", "To get feedback from others on clarity, organization, and effectiveness of your writing", "Peer review serves no useful purpose", "To replace your own writing entirely"], correct: 1, explanation: "Peer review allows writers to receive helpful feedback from others on their writing's clarity, organization, and overall effectiveness." },
    { q: "What is a helpful way to give constructive feedback during peer review?", options: ["Only pointing out flaws with no explanation", "Providing specific, balanced feedback that highlights both strengths and areas for improvement", "Giving no feedback at all", "Rewriting the entire piece yourself"], correct: 1, explanation: "Constructive feedback should be specific and balanced, noting both what works well and what could be improved." },
    { q: "Why might receiving feedback from someone other than the writer be valuable?", options: ["It has no value at all", "A fresh perspective can catch unclear points or errors the original writer might overlook", "Only the original writer's opinion matters", "Feedback always makes writing worse"], correct: 1, explanation: "A fresh set of eyes can catch unclear passages, errors, or gaps in logic that the original writer might overlook after working closely with their own text." }
