          { q: "What is 'phishing'?", options: ["A type of computer hardware", "A deceptive attempt to trick people into revealing sensitive information, often through fake emails or websites", "A programming language", "A type of internet connection"], correct: 1, explanation: "Phishing is a deceptive tactic where attackers try to trick people into revealing sensitive information, like passwords or credit card numbers, often through fake emails or websites that appear legitimate." },
          { q: "What is a common warning sign of a phishing email?", options: ["It always comes from a well-known, verified source", "Urgent language pressuring you to act quickly, along with suspicious links or requests for personal information", "Phishing emails are always clearly labeled as scams", "Phishing emails never contain any links"], correct: 1, explanation: "Phishing emails often use urgent, pressuring language and contain suspicious links or requests for personal information, trying to prompt hasty action before you think carefully." },
          { q: "What should you generally do if you receive a suspicious email asking for personal information?", options: ["Immediately provide the requested information", "Avoid clicking any links and verify the request through a separate, trusted channel", "Forward it to all your contacts", "Reply with your personal information to confirm"], correct: 1, explanation: "It's safest to avoid clicking suspicious links and instead verify the request through a separate, trusted channel, like calling the organization directly using a known phone number." },
          { q: "What is a 'firewall' used for in computing?", options: ["To physically cool down a computer", "To monitor and control incoming and outgoing network traffic based on security rules", "To compress files", "To connect to Wi-Fi"], correct: 1, explanation: "A firewall monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined security rules, helping protect a computer or network from unauthorized access." },
          { q: "How does a firewall help protect a computer or network?", options: ["It has no protective function", "It can block potentially harmful or unauthorized network traffic from reaching a computer or network", "It only works to speed up internet connections", "Firewalls only affect physical hardware, not network traffic"], correct: 1, explanation: "A firewall helps protect systems by blocking potentially harmful or unauthorized network traffic, acting as a barrier between a trusted internal network and external threats." },
          { q: "Can a firewall be either hardware-based or software-based?", options: ["No, firewalls are always physical hardware only", "Yes, firewalls can exist as dedicated hardware devices or as software running on a computer", "Firewalls don't actually exist", "Firewalls are only software installed on smartphones"], correct: 1, explanation: "Firewalls can be implemented as dedicated hardware devices, software programs running on a computer, or a combination of both." },
          { q: "What is 'two-factor authentication'?", options: ["Using only a single password for login", "A security process requiring two different forms of verification to access an account", "A type of computer virus", "A type of internet connection"], correct: 1, explanation: "Two-factor authentication requires two different forms of verification (like a password plus a code sent to your phone) to access an account, adding an extra layer of security." },
          { q: "What is a common example of a 'second factor' used in two-factor authentication?", options: ["Entering the same password twice", "A one-time code sent via text message or generated by an authentication app", "Only using your username", "Ignoring security entirely"], correct: 1, explanation: "A common second factor is a one-time code sent via text message or generated by an authentication app, which changes regularly and is separate from your password." },
          { q: "Why does two-factor authentication improve security compared to a password alone?", options: ["It doesn't actually improve security", "Even if a password is compromised, an attacker would still need the second factor to access the account", "Two-factor authentication makes accounts less secure overall", "It only works if you have no password at all"], correct: 1, explanation: "Two-factor authentication improves security because even if an attacker obtains your password, they would still need the separate second factor to actually access your account." },
          { q: "Why is it recommended to use different passwords for different accounts?", options: ["It isn't actually recommended", "If one account's password is compromised, other accounts remain protected if they use different passwords", "Using the same password everywhere is always safer", "Different passwords make accounts less secure"], correct: 1, explanation: "Using different passwords for different accounts means that if one account is compromised, your other accounts remain protected, since attackers can't reuse that same password elsewhere." },
          { q: "What generally makes a password 'strong'?", options: ["Using a short, simple word", "A longer length, combined with a mix of uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols", "Using your name and birthdate", "Using the word 'password' itself"], correct: 1, explanation: "Strong passwords are typically longer and combine a mix of uppercase letters, lowercase letters, numbers, and symbols, making them harder to guess or crack." },
          { q: "Why should you avoid using easily guessable personal information (like your birthdate) in a password?", options: ["Personal information makes passwords stronger", "Such information can often be found or guessed by others, making the password easier to crack", "There is no risk in using personal information", "Passwords should always include personal information"], correct: 1, explanation: "Easily guessable personal information (like a birthdate) can often be found or guessed by others, making a password significantly easier to crack." },
          { q: "What is 'cyberbullying'?", options: ["A type of computer hardware", "Using digital platforms to harass, threaten, or intimidate someone", "A type of internet connection", "A programming language"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cyberbullying involves using digital platforms, like social media or messaging apps, to harass, threaten, or intimidate someone." },
          { q: "What is one recommended step if you or someone you know experiences cyberbullying?", options: ["Ignore it and never tell anyone", "Save evidence (like screenshots) and report it to a trusted adult or the platform involved", "Respond with equally harmful messages", "Cyberbullying should never be reported"], correct: 1, explanation: "It's generally recommended to save evidence (like screenshots) and report cyberbullying to a trusted adult, school official, or the platform where it occurred." },
          { q: "Why might cyberbullying be particularly harmful compared to some other forms of bullying?", options: ["It has no particular unique harms", "It can happen constantly, reach a wide audience quickly, and can be difficult to escape", "Cyberbullying is always less harmful than in-person bullying", "Cyberbullying never actually reaches other people"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cyberbullying can be particularly harmful because it can happen constantly (not just at school), reach a wide audience very quickly, and can feel difficult to escape from." },
          { q: "What is a 'digital footprint'?", options: ["A type of computer hardware", "The trail of data a person leaves behind from their online activities", "A programming language", "A type of internet connection speed"], correct: 1, explanation: "A digital footprint is the trail of data and information a person leaves behind through their online activities, such as posts, searches, and website visits." },
          { q: "Why is it important to be mindful of your digital footprint?", options: ["It has no real importance", "Content shared online can be difficult to fully remove and can affect future opportunities, like college admissions or job applications", "Digital footprints automatically disappear after a short time", "Online activity has no lasting effects"], correct: 1, explanation: "Content shared online can be difficult to fully remove and may be seen by others (like college admissions officers or employers), potentially affecting future opportunities." },
          { q: "What is one way to help manage your digital footprint responsibly?", options: ["Share every detail of your life publicly with no consideration", "Think carefully before posting and regularly review your privacy settings", "Digital footprints cannot be managed at all", "Ignore all privacy settings on every platform"], correct: 1, explanation: "Thinking carefully before posting and regularly reviewing privacy settings on various platforms can help you manage your digital footprint responsibly." },
          { q: "What is the purpose of 'privacy settings' on social media and other online platforms?", options: ["They serve no real purpose", "To control who can see your information, posts, and activity on a platform", "Privacy settings only affect how a platform looks visually", "To automatically delete your account"], correct: 1, explanation: "Privacy settings allow you to control who can see your information, posts, and activity on a given platform, helping manage your online privacy." },
          { q: "Why might it be a good idea to periodically review your privacy settings on various apps and websites?", options: ["Reviewing privacy settings is never necessary", "Settings and platform policies can change over time, so periodic review helps ensure your information stays protected as intended", "Privacy settings never change once set", "Privacy settings have no real effect on your information"], correct: 1, explanation: "Since platform settings and policies can change over time, periodically reviewing your privacy settings helps ensure your information remains protected as you intend." },
          { q: "What is a general best practice regarding sharing personal information (like your home address) on public platforms?", options: ["Always share it publicly for convenience", "Be cautious about sharing sensitive personal information publicly, as it could pose safety or security risks", "Personal information has no risk when shared publicly", "Privacy settings don't affect personal information sharing"], correct: 1, explanation: "It's generally a good practice to be cautious about sharing sensitive personal information, like your home address, publicly, since it could pose safety or security risks." },
          { q: "What is a common warning sign of an online scam?", options: ["Offers that seem too good to be true, or pressure to act immediately", "Clear, verified information from an official, well-known source", "Taking plenty of time to consider a decision", "Transparent, straightforward requests with no urgency"], correct: 0, explanation: "Offers that seem too good to be true, along with pressure to act immediately, are common warning signs of an online scam." },
          { q: "What is 'misinformation'?", options: ["Only information that is intentionally created to deceive", "False or inaccurate information, regardless of whether it was intended to deceive", "Information that is always accurate", "A type of computer hardware"], correct: 1, explanation: "Misinformation refers to false or inaccurate information, which may be spread unintentionally, unlike 'disinformation,' which is spread deliberately to deceive." },
          { q: "What is one good practice for evaluating whether online information is trustworthy?", options: ["Believing everything you read online without question", "Checking multiple reputable sources and considering the original source's credibility", "Only trusting information shared by friends, regardless of its source", "There's no way to evaluate online information's trustworthiness"], correct: 1, explanation: "Checking multiple reputable sources and considering the credibility of the original source are good practices for evaluating whether online information is trustworthy." },
          { q: "You receive an urgent email claiming to be from your bank, asking you to click a link and enter your password immediately. What should you do?", options: ["Click the link and enter your information right away", "Avoid clicking the link and instead contact your bank directly through a verified phone number or website", "Reply to the email with your password", "Forward the email to all your contacts"], correct: 1, explanation: "This has classic signs of a phishing attempt — it's safest to avoid the link and verify directly with your bank through a separately verified contact method." },
          { q: "A website asks you to create an account with a password. Which password would be considered the strongest?", options: ["password123", "YourName2010", "Tr@ilBlaze92!wK", "12345678"], correct: 2, explanation: "A longer password combining uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols, without obvious personal information, is generally the strongest choice among these options." },
          { q: "What does 'https' at the start of a web address indicate?", options: ["The website is outdated", "The connection to the website is encrypted for security", "The website only contains images", "The website has no security features"], correct: 1, explanation: "'https' indicates that the connection between your browser and the website is encrypted, providing added security for data transmitted between you and the site." }
