    { q: "Why is data broken into smaller packets for transmission, rather than sent as one large piece?", options: ["There is no particular reason", "Breaking data into packets allows more efficient, reliable routing across a network, especially if some data needs retransmission", "Packets always make data transmission slower with no benefit", "Data cannot actually be sent in packets"], correct: 1, explanation: "Breaking data into smaller packets allows for more efficient, reliable routing across a network — if part of the transmission fails, only that specific packet needs retransmission, not the entire dataset." },
    { q: "What generally happens to data packets once they arrive at their destination?", options: ["They are simply discarded", "They are reassembled in the correct order to reconstruct the original data", "Packets are never reassembled", "Packets are converted into a different file format automatically"], correct: 1, explanation: "Once data packets arrive at their destination, they are reassembled in the correct order to reconstruct the original complete piece of data." }
