    { q: "What does 'comparative advantage' explain in trade?", options: ["Why countries should never trade with each other", "Why countries can benefit from specializing in producing goods they can make relatively more efficiently, then trading", "A type of government tax", "The total value of a country's exports"], correct: 1, explanation: "Comparative advantage explains why countries can benefit from trade by specializing in producing goods they can make relatively more efficiently (at a lower opportunity cost), then trading for other goods." },
    { q: "Can a country have a comparative advantage in producing a good, even if another country is more efficient at producing everything?", options: ["No, comparative advantage only applies if a country is the most efficient at everything", "Yes, comparative advantage is based on relative opportunity costs, not absolute efficiency", "Comparative advantage doesn't apply to any real-world situations", "Only the most efficient country in the world can ever benefit from trade"], correct: 1, explanation: "Yes — comparative advantage is based on relative opportunity costs, meaning a country can still benefit from specializing in what it does relatively best, even if another country is more efficient at producing everything." },
    { q: "Why might two countries choose to trade, even if one is generally more productive across the board?", options: ["There would be no reason for them to trade", "Both countries can still benefit by specializing based on comparative advantage and trading for goods produced more efficiently by the other", "Trade is only beneficial for the more productive country", "Comparative advantage guarantees identical benefits regardless of specialization"], correct: 1, explanation: "Even if one country is more productive overall, both countries can benefit by specializing according to comparative advantage, focusing on what they produce relatively most efficiently, then trading." }
