    { q: "What is the 'law of diminishing returns'?", options: ["The idea that adding more of one input always increases output at a constant rate", "The principle that, at some point, adding more of one input (while others stay fixed) yields progressively smaller increases in output", "A law requiring businesses to reduce production", "A type of government tax policy"], correct: 1, explanation: "The law of diminishing returns states that, beyond a certain point, adding more units of one input (while holding other inputs fixed) will yield progressively smaller additional increases in output." },
    { q: "What is an example that illustrates the law of diminishing returns?", options: ["Adding unlimited workers to a small kitchen, with output increasing proportionally forever", "Adding workers to a small kitchen, where each additional worker beyond a certain point contributes progressively less additional output due to limited space and equipment", "A law that only applies to government spending", "A concept unrelated to production processes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Adding more workers to a small kitchen illustrates diminishing returns — early additional workers may boost output significantly, but beyond a certain point, limited space and equipment mean each additional worker contributes progressively less." },
    { q: "Why is understanding diminishing returns useful for businesses making production decisions?", options: ["It has no practical use for businesses", "It helps businesses determine an efficient level of input use, avoiding adding resources well past the point of meaningful benefit", "Diminishing returns means businesses should always add unlimited inputs", "This concept only applies to government policy, not businesses"], correct: 1, explanation: "Understanding diminishing returns helps businesses determine an efficient level of input use, avoiding continuing to add resources well past the point where each additional unit contributes meaningfully to output." }
