    { q: "Which of these is an example of using probability in everyday life?", options: ["Reading a book for entertainment", "Estimating the chance of catching a flight on time based on traffic patterns", "Cooking a meal following a recipe exactly", "Watering a houseplant on a fixed schedule"], correct: 1, explanation: "Estimating the chance of catching a flight based on traffic patterns is an example of using probability-based reasoning in everyday decision-making." },
    { q: "How might understanding probability help someone make more informed everyday decisions?", options: ["Probability has no practical use outside of math class", "It can help people weigh likely outcomes and risks when making choices under uncertainty", "Probability only applies to games of chance, never real decisions", "Understanding probability guarantees a specific favorable outcome every time"], correct: 1, explanation: "Understanding probability can help people weigh likely outcomes and risks when making choices under uncertainty, such as deciding whether to bring an umbrella or how to plan around potential delays." },
    { q: "A commuter checks a traffic app that shows a 'likely delay' based on historical data for that time of day. What underlying concept does this rely on?", options: ["A fixed guarantee of delay", "Probability estimates based on patterns and historical data", "A random guess with no data involved", "This has nothing to do with probability"], correct: 1, explanation: "This relies on probability estimates based on historical traffic patterns and data, helping predict the likelihood of a delay rather than guaranteeing one." }
