    { q: "Why might insurance companies use data from large groups of people, rather than just individual cases, to estimate probabilities?", options: ["Large groups of data provide no more useful information than a single individual case", "Analyzing patterns across large groups can provide more statistically reliable estimates of overall likelihood than looking at just one person", "Insurance companies are legally required to ignore all statistical data", "Using large groups makes the calculations less accurate"], correct: 1, explanation: "Analyzing patterns across large groups of people can provide more statistically reliable estimates of overall likelihood, since a single individual case doesn't reveal broader patterns the way a large dataset can." },
    { q: "What is a general reason people might choose to purchase insurance, from a probability perspective?", options: ["Insurance eliminates all element of risk and uncertainty completely", "To help manage financial risk from relatively unlikely but potentially costly events", "Insurance has no relationship to probability or risk management concepts", "Purchasing insurance guarantees a financial profit"], correct: 1, explanation: "People often purchase insurance to help manage financial risk from events that may be relatively unlikely but could be quite costly if they occur, effectively spreading and reducing individual risk exposure." }
