    { q: "What is a common misunderstanding about correlation, sometimes confused with probability-related conclusions?", options: ["Correlation and causation are always exactly the same thing", "Assuming that because two things are correlated (they tend to occur together), one must be causing the other", "There is no distinction to be made between correlation and causation", "Correlation always proves a probability of exactly 1"], correct: 1, explanation: "A common misunderstanding is assuming that correlation implies causation — just because two things tend to occur together statistically doesn't necessarily mean one is causing the other." },
    { q: "Why is it a mistake to assume a rare event 'can't happen to you' simply because it's statistically unlikely?", options: ["Rare events are actually impossible and truly never occur", "Even low-probability events can and do occur to specific individuals — a low probability doesn't mean zero probability", "Statistically unlikely events always have a probability of exactly 0", "This assumption is actually mathematically correct"], correct: 1, explanation: "It's a mistake because even low-probability events can and do occur to specific individuals — a low probability is not the same as a zero probability, so rare events remain genuinely possible." }
