        name: "Strong Passwords",
  questions: [
    { q: "What makes a password strong?", options: ["Being short and easy to remember, like a common word", "Being long, complex, and unique, combining letters, numbers, and symbols in a way that's hard to guess", "Using the same simple password across every account", "Using your name or birthdate"], correct: 1, explanation: "A strong password is generally long, complex, and unique, combining letters, numbers, and symbols in a way that makes it difficult for others to guess or crack." },
    { q: "Why is it generally considered risky to reuse the same password across multiple accounts?", options: ["Reusing passwords has no security implications at all", "If one account is compromised, a reused password could potentially allow access to all other accounts using the same password", "Reusing passwords always makes accounts more secure", "There is no relationship between password reuse and account security"], correct: 1, explanation: "If one account using a particular password is compromised, a reused password could potentially allow unauthorized access to all other accounts sharing that same password, making reuse a significant security risk." },
    { q: "What is one tool commonly recommended to help manage multiple strong, unique passwords?", options: ["Writing every password on a single publicly visible note", "A password manager, which can securely store and generate unique passwords for different accounts", "Using the exact same password everywhere for simplicity", "There is no tool that can help with this challenge"], correct: 1, explanation: "A password manager is a commonly recommended tool that can securely store and even generate unique, strong passwords for different accounts, making it easier to avoid password reuse." }
  ]
