    { q: "Why should you be cautious about public Wi-Fi (like at a coffee shop)?", options: ["Public Wi-Fi is always perfectly secure with no risks whatsoever", "Public networks can potentially be less secure, making it easier for others on the same network to intercept unencrypted data", "There is no difference between public and private Wi-Fi networks", "Public Wi-Fi networks never allow any device to connect"], correct: 1, explanation: "Public Wi-Fi networks can potentially be less secure than private networks, since it may be easier for others on the same public network to intercept unencrypted data being transmitted." },
    { q: "What is one commonly recommended precaution when needing to access sensitive accounts (like banking) while using public Wi-Fi?", options: ["There are no useful precautions for this situation", "Using a VPN (Virtual Private Network) to help encrypt your connection, or avoiding sensitive activities on public Wi-Fi altogether", "Always sharing your banking password over public Wi-Fi networks", "Public Wi-Fi has no relevance to sensitive account security"], correct: 1, explanation: "A commonly recommended precaution is using a VPN (Virtual Private Network) to help encrypt your connection on public Wi-Fi, or simply avoiding especially sensitive activities, like banking, while connected to public networks." },
    { q: "What does connecting to a website using 'HTTPS' (rather than just 'HTTP') generally indicate, which is relevant when considering network security?", options: ["HTTPS indicates the website is loading unusually slowly", "HTTPS indicates the connection to that specific website is encrypted, adding a layer of protection even on a potentially less secure network", "There is no meaningful difference between HTTPS and HTTP", "HTTPS means a website is guaranteed to be completely trustworthy in every respect"], correct: 1, explanation: "HTTPS indicates that the connection to that specific website is encrypted, which adds a layer of protection for data being transmitted to and from that site, even when using a potentially less secure network like public Wi-Fi." }
