    { q: "Why do programmers plan out steps before writing code?", options: ["Planning is unnecessary and always wastes time", "Planning ahead can help identify potential issues and clarify the overall approach before investing time in writing detailed code", "Programmers never plan anything before writing code", "Planning is only useful for very simple programs with no complexity"], correct: 1, explanation: "Programmers plan out steps before writing code because doing so can help identify potential issues early and clarify the overall approach, which can save time and reduce errors compared to writing code without a clear plan." },
    { q: "What is one potential risk of jumping straight into writing code without first planning an approach?", options: ["There is no risk at all in skipping the planning stage", "A programmer might realize partway through that their approach has significant flaws, requiring substantial rework", "Skipping planning always results in a faster, more efficient solution with no downsides", "Planning has no relationship to the quality of the final code produced"], correct: 1, explanation: "A potential risk of skipping the planning stage is that a programmer might realize partway through writing code that their approach has significant flaws, which could require substantial rework that might have been avoided with earlier planning." },
    { q: "What is one common planning tool, besides pseudocode, that programmers might use to visually map out a program's logic?", options: ["Planning tools do not exist in programming", "A flowchart, which visually represents the steps and decision points in a process", "A random number generator", "A type of programming language error message"], correct: 1, explanation: "A flowchart is a common planning tool that programmers might use to visually represent the steps and decision points in a process, helping to map out logic before writing actual code." }
