    { q: "You're tasked with building a large mobile app. Instead of trying to build the whole thing at once, you break it into pieces: the login screen, the main feed, and the settings page. What concept does this illustrate?", options: ["An algorithm", "Decomposition", "A loop", "Abstraction"], correct: 1, explanation: "Breaking the large app-building task into smaller, distinct pieces illustrates decomposition." },
    { q: "Before writing actual code, a programmer writes out the general logic in plain English: 'Check if the user is logged in. If not, show the login screen. If so, show the main feed.' What is this an example of?", options: ["A finished, executable program", "Pseudocode", "An infinite loop", "A type of computer hardware"], correct: 1, explanation: "Describing the logic in plain, human-readable language before writing actual code is an example of pseudocode." },
    { q: "A program isn't behaving as expected, so a programmer tests small sections of the code individually to figure out exactly where the problem is occurring. What process does this describe?", options: ["Decomposition", "Debugging", "Abstraction", "Pattern recognition"], correct: 1, explanation: "Testing small sections of code individually to find and fix an error describes the process of debugging." }
