    { q: "What is a 'nested loop' in programming?", options: ["A loop that never actually runs any code", "A loop placed inside another loop, where the inner loop fully completes its repetitions for each single repetition of the outer loop", "A type of conditional statement with no relationship to loops", "A loop that can only ever run a single time"], correct: 1, explanation: "A nested loop is a loop placed inside another loop, where the inner loop completes all of its repetitions for each single repetition of the outer loop." },
    { q: "If an outer loop runs 3 times, and for each of those times, an inner nested loop runs 4 times, how many total times would the inner loop's code execute?", options: ["7 times", "12 times", "3 times", "4 times"], correct: 1, explanation: "Since the inner loop runs 4 times for each of the outer loop's 3 repetitions, the inner loop's code executes a total of 3 × 4 = 12 times." },
    { q: "Which of these scenarios would most likely be represented using a nested loop?", options: ["Printing a single number one time", "Printing a multiplication table, where for each row number, you print the product with every column number", "Checking a single true or false condition", "Storing one single value in a variable"], correct: 1, explanation: "Printing a multiplication table would most likely use a nested loop, since for each row (outer loop), you need to calculate and print a value for every column (inner loop)." }
