          { q: "Why are loops commonly used together with arrays (or lists) in programming?", options: ["Loops and arrays have no meaningful relationship to each other", "A loop can efficiently process or access each item in an array, one at a time, without needing to write separate code for every single item", "Arrays can never actually be used together with any type of loop", "Using a loop with an array always causes a program to produce an error"], correct: 1, explanation: "Loops are commonly used together with arrays because a loop can efficiently process or access each item in an array, one at a time, without requiring separate, individually written code for every single item in the array." },
          { q: "If you have an array `names` containing 5 items, and you want to print every name, which approach would generally be most practical?", options: ["Writing 5 separate print statements, each one hardcoded for a specific name", "Using a loop that goes through the array and prints each item, one at a time", "It would be impossible to print every item in an array under any circumstances", "Arrays cannot be used with print statements of any kind"], correct: 1, explanation: "Using a loop to go through the array and print each item is generally the most practical approach, since it works efficiently regardless of how many items are actually in the array, without needing separate hardcoded statements." },
          { q: "If an array has 8 items, and you use a loop to go through each item exactly once, how many times would the loop's code run?", options: ["7 times", "8 times", "9 times", "1 time"], correct: 1, explanation: "If a loop goes through each item in an array exactly once, and the array has 8 items, the loop's code would run 8 times, once for each item." },
          { q: "What does a 'break' statement typically do within a loop?", options: ["It causes the loop to restart from the very beginning", "It immediately stops the loop entirely, exiting out of it before its normal stopping condition would otherwise be reached", "A 'break' statement has no actual effect on a loop", "It permanently pauses the entire program"], correct: 1, explanation: "A 'break' statement typically stops the loop entirely and exits out of it immediately, even if the loop's normal stopping condition hasn't been reached yet." },
          { q: "What does a 'continue' statement typically do within a loop?", options: ["It stops the entire loop completely, similar to 'break'", "It skips the rest of the current repetition of the loop and moves on directly to the next one", "A 'continue' statement has no actual effect on a loop", "It causes the loop to run one additional, extra time"], correct: 1, explanation: "A 'continue' statement typically skips the rest of the code for the current repetition of the loop and moves directly on to the next repetition, without necessarily stopping the loop entirely." },
          { q: "If you wanted a loop to stop searching through a list immediately once it finds a specific item, which statement would be most useful?", options: ["A 'continue' statement", "A 'break' statement", "Neither statement would be useful in this scenario", "This scenario has no relationship to loop control statements"], correct: 1, explanation: "A 'break' statement would be most useful in this scenario, since it allows the loop to stop entirely and exit immediately once the specific item has been found, rather than needlessly continuing to check the remaining items." },
          { q: "If you wanted to print \"Hi\" exactly 3 times, which approach makes sense?", options: ["Writing a loop that runs \"for each number from 1 to 3\", printing \"Hi\" each time", "Writing a loop that runs infinitely with no stopping condition", "Printing \"Hi\" only a single time, since loops cannot repeat an exact number of times", "This task cannot be accomplished using any type of loop"], correct: 0, explanation: "Writing a loop that runs for each number from 1 to 3, printing \"Hi\" during each repetition, would correctly print \"Hi\" exactly 3 times." },
          { q: "A loop is set up as `for (let i = 0; i < 4; i = i + 1)`. How many total times will this loop's code run?", options: ["3 times", "4 times", "5 times", "0 times"], correct: 1, explanation: "Starting at i=0 and continuing while i < 4 (incrementing by 1 each time), the loop runs when i is 0, 1, 2, and 3 — a total of 4 times — before stopping once i reaches 4." },
          { q: "An outer loop runs 2 times, and a nested inner loop runs 5 times for each repetition of the outer loop. What is the total number of times the inner loop's code executes?", options: ["7 times", "10 times", "2 times", "5 times"], correct: 1, explanation: "Since the inner loop runs 5 times for each of the outer loop's 2 repetitions, the inner loop's code executes a total of 2 × 5 = 10 times." }
