    { q: "In the function definition `function greet(name) { print(\"Hello, \" + name); }`, what role does `name` play?", options: ["It is the fixed, unchangeable output of the function", "It is a parameter, representing an input value that can vary each time the function is called", "It is a type of programming language error", "It is a comment with no functional effect on the code"], correct: 1, explanation: "In this function, `name` is a parameter — a placeholder representing an input value that can be different each time the `greet` function is called, allowing the greeting to be personalized." },
    { q: "Why are parameters useful for making functions more flexible and reusable?", options: ["Parameters have no effect on a function's flexibility", "They allow the same function to work with different specific input values, rather than the function only ever working with one single, fixed value", "Parameters always make functions less useful and more restrictive", "Functions cannot actually use parameters at all"], correct: 1, explanation: "Parameters are useful because they allow the same function to work with different specific input values each time it's called, rather than the function only ever being able to work with one single, hardcoded, fixed value." }
